;; spec:   syntax §4.4, §4.5 (not an ownership rule; found probing macro loops)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 3
;; audit:  clean
;; syntax §4.4: "(for-each (range a b) (fn (i) body)), with a literal
;; (range ..) and a literal fn, ⟹ (let ((s a) (m b)) (loop ((i s)) ...))
;; ... the body spliced in with no closure"; "any other for-each is the
;; library function (§4.5)". A fn whose parameter carries a type
;; annotation is still a literal fn (syntax §3.2: param ::= sym | sym:
;; type), so this must expand to the counting loop and return 0+1+2 = 3.
;;
;; Observed (fibref run):
;;   rejected:
;;   006-for-each-range-annotated-fn.fib:28:19: range takes 1 argument(s), got 2
;; Diagnosis: expand (crates/fibref/src/expand/prelude/loops.rs,
;; literal_fn accepts only an unannotated parameter) together with the
;; prelude: lib/prelude.fib defines (range n) with one argument, so a
;; two-argument (range a b) exists only inside this one macro shape, and
;; every for-each over (range a b) that misses the shape (an annotated
;; parameter, a named fn, a let-bound f) is a type error instead of a
;; library call. The spec never says which arity range has. Related
;; (spec: no hygiene, so by the letter correct, but surprising): a
;; let-bound local named range is ignored by the expansion:
;;   (let ((c (cell 0)) (range (fn (a b) (conj [] 100))))
;;     (do (for-each (range 0 3) (fn (i) (set! c (+ @c i)))) @c))  => 3
(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((c (cell 0)))
    (do (for-each (range 0 3) (fn (i: i64) (set! c (+ @c i))))
        @c)))
